1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,666
Bombay.

2
00:00:00,874 --> 00:00:01,999
Nineteen twenty four.

3
00:00:02,387 --> 00:00:05,635
A man walks into a bank, carrying his wife's diamond.

4
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:08,103
Two hundred and forty five carats.

5
00:00:08,397 --> 00:00:11,364
More than twice the size of the Kohinoor, as it is today.

6
00:00:11,805 --> 00:00:12,963
Is he here to sell it?

7
00:00:13,246 --> 00:00:13,729
No.

8
00:00:13,998 --> 00:00:16,830
He wants a loan, with the diamond as the guarantee.

9
00:00:17,181 --> 00:00:20,731
Ask any Indian family what it means to pledge the house gold.

10
00:00:20,871 --> 00:00:22,433
It's the last thing you do.

11
00:00:22,633 --> 00:00:24,411
When there is nothing else left.

12
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:26,180
So why is he doing it?

13
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:34,184
Because more than fourteen hundred kilometres away, in a steel town cut out of the jungle, thousands of workers are waiting for their wages.

14
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:37,674
And the company that pays them is almost out of money.

15
00:00:37,975 --> 00:00:39,647
You may never have heard his name.

16
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:41,385
Sir Dorabji Tata.

17
00:00:41,601 --> 00:00:43,574
The company is Tata Steel.

18
00:00:43,886 --> 00:00:45,210
Now look around you today.

19
00:00:45,494 --> 00:00:46,934
The salt in your kitchen.

20
00:00:46,886 --> 00:00:48,159
The tea in your cup.

21
00:00:48,206 --> 00:00:49,619
The steel in our bridges.

22
00:00:49,815 --> 00:00:51,636
Maybe even the plane over your head.

23
00:00:52,019 --> 00:00:52,560
Tata.

24
00:00:52,864 --> 00:00:57,169
And yet you won't find a single Tata at the top of any rich list.

25
00:00:57,356 --> 00:00:58,509
How is that possible?

26
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:00,704
The answer is in this bank.

27
00:01:00,831 --> 00:01:02,641
In what happens to that diamond.

28
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:08,119
A choice this man makes, eight years later, still shapes who controls Tata today.

29
00:01:08,321 --> 00:01:12,298
And in twenty twenty six, that choice has turned into a fight.

30
00:01:12,509 --> 00:01:18,745
But to understand why a man would pawn his wife's diamond for a steel plant, we have to go back to the beginning.

31
00:01:18,938 --> 00:01:21,124
And the beginning is not what you think.

32
00:01:21,256 --> 00:01:22,394
It's not a palace.

33
00:01:22,546 --> 00:01:26,137
It's a family of priests, in a small town in Gujarat.

34
00:01:27,497 --> 00:01:28,323
Navsari.

35
00:01:28,508 --> 00:01:29,761
Eighteen thirty nine.

36
00:01:30,032 --> 00:01:33,225
A boy is born into a family of Parsi priests.

37
00:01:33,415 --> 00:01:34,661
A respected family.

38
00:01:34,894 --> 00:01:36,201
But not a rich one.

39
00:01:36,505 --> 00:01:37,850
You already know his name.

40
00:01:38,062 --> 00:01:39,378
Jamsetji Tata.

41
00:01:39,711 --> 00:01:43,476
For generations, the men of this family have served as priests.

42
00:01:43,730 --> 00:01:46,885
His grandfather moved to Bombay and kept a small shop there.

43
00:01:47,138 --> 00:01:50,253
And his father does something the family has never done.

44
00:01:50,574 --> 00:01:53,796
He walks away from the priesthood, and goes into business.

45
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:55,585
Think about that for a second.

46
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:00,458
In most Indian homes, even today, the safe path is the respected path.

47
00:02:00,738 --> 00:02:02,723
His father chose the risky one.

48
00:02:03,022 --> 00:02:04,596
So what does the boy have?

49
00:02:04,931 --> 00:02:05,688
Not money.

50
00:02:05,863 --> 00:02:07,499
A sharp head for numbers.

51
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:10,921
He can do big sums in his mind, and his parents notice.

52
00:02:11,099 --> 00:02:13,559
So they find a way to get him an education.

53
00:02:13,768 --> 00:02:16,086
You know what that means in an Indian family, don't you?

54
00:02:16,255 --> 00:02:17,853
The fees come first.

55
00:02:18,103 --> 00:02:22,685
At fourteen, he joins his father in Bombay and studies at Elphinstone College.

56
00:02:22,856 --> 00:02:26,373
Soon after, he's working in his father's small trading business.

57
00:02:26,473 --> 00:02:31,379
Learning on the job, far from home, opening a branch in Shanghai, in China.

58
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:32,463
It's going well.

59
00:02:32,737 --> 00:02:35,571
And then, almost everything falls apart.

60
00:02:35,864 --> 00:02:37,129
Eighteen sixty four.

61
00:02:37,393 --> 00:02:42,764
Jamsetji sails to England, carrying papers that say he will be paid for a big load of cotton.

62
00:02:42,964 --> 00:02:46,545
But by the time his ship docks, cotton prices have crashed.

63
00:02:46,742 --> 00:02:48,595
The papers are worthless.

64
00:02:49,114 --> 00:02:51,335
His father's firm cannot go on.

65
00:02:51,608 --> 00:02:59,391
Still in his twenties, Jamsetji has to close it down, face every debt, and slowly win back the trust of the people they owe.

66
00:02:59,784 --> 00:03:02,673
Isn't that the kind of failure most of us are scared of?

67
00:03:02,987 --> 00:03:05,330
The kind people talk about for years.

68
00:03:05,722 --> 00:03:08,667
He comes home, and Bombay is in deep trouble.

69
00:03:08,936 --> 00:03:09,980
Banks failing.

70
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:11,397
Merchants ruined.

71
00:03:11,639 --> 00:03:13,778
The Tatas could have vanished right there.

72
00:03:14,085 --> 00:03:16,647
One more family wiped out by a crash.

73
00:03:16,955 --> 00:03:17,795
Do they give up?

74
00:03:18,069 --> 00:03:18,582
No.

75
00:03:18,835 --> 00:03:22,191
His father wins a big contract, supplying the British army.

76
00:03:22,412 --> 00:03:24,558
That puts the family back on its feet.

77
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:29,079
And in eighteen sixty eight, at twenty nine, Jamsetji starts again.

78
00:03:29,325 --> 00:03:30,323
His own firm.

79
00:03:30,604 --> 00:03:32,453
Twenty one thousand rupees.

80
00:03:32,701 --> 00:03:35,220
If you've ever failed, hold on to this.

81
00:03:35,384 --> 00:03:38,135
The story of Tata doesn't begin with a fortune.

82
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:44,167
It begins with a priest's son whose family nearly lost everything, and who started again.

83
00:03:44,518 --> 00:03:46,210
Now look at Bombay in those years.

84
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:50,431
A city of ships and cotton, and most of the money flows one way.

85
00:03:50,754 --> 00:03:51,267
Out.

86
00:03:51,479 --> 00:03:53,896
Indian cotton sails to the mills of England.

87
00:03:54,030 --> 00:03:56,670
The cloth comes back, and Indians pay for it.

88
00:03:56,747 --> 00:03:59,837
Jamsetji has seen those English mills with his own eyes.

89
00:04:00,049 --> 00:04:02,189
And one question won't leave him alone.

90
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:05,443
Why send our cotton away, when we can spin it right here?

91
00:04:05,653 --> 00:04:11,886
Like many traders of his time, his family's business ran on cotton and on opium, sold to China.

92
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:15,490
That is part of this story too, and it's worth saying out loud.

93
00:04:15,992 --> 00:04:17,996
Then he builds a mill of his own.

94
00:04:18,255 --> 00:04:20,568
Not in Bombay, where everyone builds.

95
00:04:20,746 --> 00:04:23,540
In Nagpur, right where the cotton grows.

96
00:04:23,829 --> 00:04:28,204
It opens on the very day Queen Victoria is declared Empress of India.

97
00:04:28,504 --> 00:04:30,530
So he names it Empress Mills.

98
00:04:30,846 --> 00:04:33,478
And then he does something strange for his time.

99
00:04:33,809 --> 00:04:36,210
He starts a pension fund for his workers.

100
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:39,310
Later, he pays them when they're hurt on the job.

101
00:04:39,553 --> 00:04:41,193
Think about your own first job.

102
00:04:41,508 --> 00:04:43,355
Did anyone promise you a pension?

103
00:04:43,628 --> 00:04:45,045
Was it pure kindness?

104
00:04:45,347 --> 00:04:48,291
He answered that himself, in eighteen ninety five.

105
00:04:48,629 --> 00:04:51,193
He was no kinder than anyone else, he said.

106
00:04:51,351 --> 00:04:54,877
But healthy workers were the sure foundation of his success.

107
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:58,371
Now, you've probably heard the most famous Tata story.

108
00:04:58,642 --> 00:05:02,318
Jamsetji is turned away from a whites only hotel in Bombay.

109
00:05:02,551 --> 00:05:05,407
So he builds the Taj, to answer the insult.

110
00:05:05,645 --> 00:05:07,250
It's a great story, isn't it?

111
00:05:07,623 --> 00:05:11,337
But historians have found no record that it ever happened.

112
00:05:11,568 --> 00:05:14,277
What is on record is the hotel itself.

113
00:05:14,538 --> 00:05:18,321
It opens in December, nineteen oh three, on the Bombay waterfront.

114
00:05:18,515 --> 00:05:20,467
Designed by an Indian architect.

115
00:05:20,621 --> 00:05:22,291
Lit by electricity.

116
00:05:22,551 --> 00:05:23,765
Hold on to this hotel.

117
00:05:23,889 --> 00:05:27,447
It comes back in this story, on the worst night of its life.

118
00:05:27,702 --> 00:05:30,026
But a hotel was never his biggest dream.

119
00:05:30,306 --> 00:05:32,567
Jamsetji had three bigger ones.

120
00:05:32,693 --> 00:05:36,529
A steel plant, so India could make its own rails and bridges.

121
00:05:36,667 --> 00:05:39,592
Power from the monsoon rains of the Western Ghats.

122
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:44,879
And a world class institute of science, so Indians could invent, not just buy.

123
00:05:45,394 --> 00:05:49,927
For the institute alone, he offers property worth thirty lakh rupees.

124
00:05:50,248 --> 00:05:52,486
The hardest of the three is steel.

125
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:58,742
Because to make steel, you need iron ore, the right coal and water, all in one place.

126
00:05:59,013 --> 00:06:02,083
And for years, every place he tries is wrong.

127
00:06:03,771 --> 00:06:06,469
One site has ore, but the coal is useless.

128
00:06:06,692 --> 00:06:08,758
Another has coal, but no water.

129
00:06:09,238 --> 00:06:12,899
Then, in February nineteen oh four, a letter reaches him.

130
00:06:13,216 --> 00:06:16,769
It's from an Indian geologist, a man who studies rocks.

131
00:06:17,042 --> 00:06:18,480
Pramatha Nath Bose.

132
00:06:18,750 --> 00:06:22,170
He has been walking the hills of Mayurbhanj, in today's Odisha.

133
00:06:22,437 --> 00:06:24,357
And he has found iron ore.

134
00:06:24,558 --> 00:06:25,612
A lot of it.

135
00:06:25,877 --> 00:06:29,502
This letter will lead them to the ore that feeds Tata Steel.

136
00:06:29,830 --> 00:06:34,382
But three months later, on a trip to Germany, Jamsetji Tata dies.

137
00:06:34,708 --> 00:06:36,270
He never sees those hills.

138
00:06:36,477 --> 00:06:37,601
No steel plant.

139
00:06:37,652 --> 00:06:38,883
No power company.

140
00:06:38,937 --> 00:06:39,969
No institute.

141
00:06:40,365 --> 00:06:42,049
So who carries the dream now?

142
00:06:42,388 --> 00:06:43,066
His son.

143
00:06:43,245 --> 00:06:44,026
Dorabji.

144
00:06:44,298 --> 00:06:46,860
And Dorabji does not run it from an office.

145
00:06:47,005 --> 00:06:51,412
He has already been out in the jungle himself, with the men searching for iron.

146
00:06:51,666 --> 00:06:55,065
Now they go back, looking for one perfect spot.

147
00:06:55,305 --> 00:06:59,763
They find it near a small village called Sakchi, right where two rivers meet.

148
00:07:00,041 --> 00:07:00,938
Ore nearby.

149
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:01,982
Coal nearby.

150
00:07:02,134 --> 00:07:03,450
Water all year.

151
00:07:03,731 --> 00:07:05,175
Now they need money.

152
00:07:05,406 --> 00:07:06,510
A lot of money.

153
00:07:06,736 --> 00:07:08,196
Where do you even find it?

154
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:09,845
They try London first.

155
00:07:10,006 --> 00:07:11,516
The answer is cold.

156
00:07:11,732 --> 00:07:15,139
So in nineteen oh seven, the Tatas take a huge risk.

157
00:07:15,384 --> 00:07:16,710
They ask India.

158
00:07:18,096 --> 00:07:20,072
Bombay, nineteen oh seven.

159
00:07:20,331 --> 00:07:21,838
The share office opens.

160
00:07:22,068 --> 00:07:23,114
And the people come.

161
00:07:23,471 --> 00:07:24,141
Who are they?

162
00:07:24,473 --> 00:07:26,570
One man who was there wrote it down.

163
00:07:26,883 --> 00:07:28,514
Old and young, he said.

164
00:07:28,709 --> 00:07:29,916
Rich and poor.

165
00:07:30,130 --> 00:07:31,329
Men and women.

166
00:07:31,592 --> 00:07:33,755
They came, offering their mites.

167
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:34,794
Their mites.

168
00:07:34,961 --> 00:07:36,109
Their few coins.

169
00:07:36,159 --> 00:07:37,510
Their small savings.

170
00:07:37,748 --> 00:07:42,700
In a matter of weeks, about eight thousand Indians put up the entire capital.

171
00:07:42,992 --> 00:07:45,110
Two crore thirty one lakh rupees.

172
00:07:45,505 --> 00:07:47,749
Now imagine you're standing in that line.

173
00:07:47,998 --> 00:07:49,676
India is ruled by Britain.

174
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:54,621
You've saved this money slowly, maybe for a daughter's wedding, maybe for old age.

175
00:07:54,817 --> 00:07:58,283
And you're about to bet it that Indians can make steel.

176
00:07:58,661 --> 00:07:59,566
Would you have done it?

177
00:07:59,809 --> 00:08:01,449
Not everyone believed, of course.

178
00:08:01,579 --> 00:08:08,262
According to Tata's own histories, a senior British railway official, Sir Frederick Upcott, laughed at the idea.

179
00:08:08,518 --> 00:08:12,442
He said he would eat every pound of steel rail they managed to make.

180
00:08:12,735 --> 00:08:16,133
So how do you turn a hill of red rock into steel?

181
00:08:16,435 --> 00:08:18,528
You build a tower of fire.

182
00:08:18,782 --> 00:08:22,270
Iron ore, limestone and baked coal go in at the top.

183
00:08:22,469 --> 00:08:24,504
Hot air blasts in at the bottom.

184
00:08:24,616 --> 00:08:27,554
And out pours iron, glowing like lava.

185
00:08:27,761 --> 00:08:30,639
The first iron flows in December nineteen eleven.

186
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:32,796
The first steel, in nineteen twelve.

187
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:35,090
Then the First World War comes.

188
00:08:35,265 --> 00:08:40,664
Britain's armies need rails, and many of them come from Tata, by Tata's own records.

189
00:08:40,848 --> 00:08:45,417
Years later, as the Tata story goes, Dorabji had a reply for Upcott.

190
00:08:45,588 --> 00:08:49,277
If he had kept his promise, he would have had some indigestion.

191
00:08:49,787 --> 00:08:52,722
In nineteen nineteen, the town gets a new name.

192
00:08:53,001 --> 00:08:53,957
Jamshedpur.

193
00:08:54,277 --> 00:08:56,188
After the man who never saw it.

194
00:08:56,435 --> 00:08:58,178
His other dreams come true too.

195
00:08:58,418 --> 00:09:00,389
The institute opens in Bangalore.

196
00:09:00,601 --> 00:09:03,387
Today it's the Indian Institute of Science.

197
00:09:03,556 --> 00:09:07,555
And monsoon lakes in the Western Ghats send power to Bombay's mills.

198
00:09:07,779 --> 00:09:11,432
So the dreamer is gone, but every dream is alive.

199
00:09:11,770 --> 00:09:13,737
It should be a happy ending, shouldn't it?

200
00:09:13,993 --> 00:09:14,704
It isn't.

201
00:09:16,289 --> 00:09:18,490
A steel town is no fairy tale.

202
00:09:18,719 --> 00:09:21,588
In nineteen twenty two, the workers go on strike.

203
00:09:21,790 --> 00:09:23,540
Then the good years end.

204
00:09:23,733 --> 00:09:24,771
Prices fall.

205
00:09:24,868 --> 00:09:27,416
Cheap iron and steel pour in from abroad.

206
00:09:27,573 --> 00:09:34,022
By nineteen twenty four, the company needs close to two crore rupees, to pay its wages and the debts it owes.

207
00:09:34,229 --> 00:09:36,757
Where do you find that kind of money, fast?

208
00:09:36,912 --> 00:09:38,414
The banks want a guarantee.

209
00:09:38,595 --> 00:09:39,753
Something valuable.

210
00:09:39,899 --> 00:09:42,319
So we're back in that bank in Bombay.

211
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:45,231
The diamond on the counter is called the Jubilee.

212
00:09:45,363 --> 00:09:50,994
It was found in South Africa in eighteen ninety five, and named for Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee.

213
00:09:51,155 --> 00:09:54,781
Around nineteen hundred, Dorabji bought it for his wife, Meherbai.

214
00:09:54,928 --> 00:09:57,882
She wore it at royal courts and grand occasions.

215
00:09:58,038 --> 00:10:04,204
Now Dorabji hands it over as the guarantee, with the rest of her jewellery, to the Imperial Bank of India.

216
00:10:04,371 --> 00:10:07,023
Today you know that bank as the State Bank of India.

217
00:10:07,210 --> 00:10:08,446
The loan comes through.

218
00:10:08,599 --> 00:10:10,078
The furnaces stay lit.

219
00:10:10,191 --> 00:10:11,623
The workers get paid.

220
00:10:11,788 --> 00:10:13,060
It was her diamond.

221
00:10:13,218 --> 00:10:18,542
And it went into a bank, so that families fourteen hundred kilometres away could eat.

222
00:10:19,045 --> 00:10:20,653
Did they ever get it back?

223
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:21,570
Yes.

224
00:10:21,824 --> 00:10:26,014
We know because, eight years later, it shows up in Dorabji's will.

225
00:10:26,356 --> 00:10:30,846
In nineteen thirty one, Meherbai dies of leukaemia, a cancer of the blood.

226
00:10:31,166 --> 00:10:33,375
The next year, Dorabji dies too.

227
00:10:33,656 --> 00:10:34,865
They have no children.

228
00:10:35,166 --> 00:10:37,492
And here is the turn in this whole story.

229
00:10:37,735 --> 00:10:40,502
He leaves everything to a charitable trust.

230
00:10:40,682 --> 00:10:42,168
His shares in Tata Sons.

231
00:10:42,266 --> 00:10:43,207
His property.

232
00:10:43,463 --> 00:10:47,854
And twenty one pieces of Meherbai's jewellery, the Jubilee among them.

233
00:10:48,052 --> 00:10:50,323
The Sir Dorabji Tata Trust.

234
00:10:50,631 --> 00:10:53,563
A few years later, the trust sells the jewels.

235
00:10:53,739 --> 00:10:57,404
And that money helps build a hospital for the disease that took her.

236
00:10:57,668 --> 00:11:02,815
The Tata Memorial Hospital, for cancer, opens in Bombay in nineteen forty one.

237
00:11:03,013 --> 00:11:05,503
So the diamond helped build a hospital.

238
00:11:05,701 --> 00:11:12,860
If you, or someone you love, has ever been treated at Tata Memorial, you've been inside what that diamond helped build.

239
00:11:13,154 --> 00:11:14,740
But the shares in Tata Sons?

240
00:11:14,945 --> 00:11:16,622
Those the trust keeps.

241
00:11:16,805 --> 00:11:19,079
And that means something no one had planned.

242
00:11:19,258 --> 00:11:22,557
A charity now owns a big piece of the empire.

243
00:11:22,754 --> 00:11:23,714
Keep that in mind.

244
00:11:23,803 --> 00:11:25,271
It's the key to the ending.

245
00:11:26,628 --> 00:11:28,894
Now the story needs a new captain.

246
00:11:29,081 --> 00:11:30,812
And this one flies.

247
00:11:31,105 --> 00:11:34,525
His full name is long, so everyone just calls him J.R.D.

248
00:11:34,919 --> 00:11:36,109
He was a Tata too.

249
00:11:36,384 --> 00:11:40,525
Born in Paris, to a French mother, he served a year in the French army.

250
00:11:40,786 --> 00:11:44,833
But when he joined Tata in nineteen twenty five, he didn't start as a boss.

251
00:11:44,981 --> 00:11:47,683
He started as an apprentice, without pay.

252
00:11:48,076 --> 00:11:52,043
In nineteen twenty nine, he gets India's first pilot licence.

253
00:11:52,552 --> 00:11:55,161
The fifteenth of October, nineteen thirty two.

254
00:11:55,469 --> 00:11:56,114
Karachi.

255
00:11:56,374 --> 00:11:57,856
Six thirty in the morning.

256
00:11:58,281 --> 00:12:01,326
J.R.D. climbs into a tiny single engine plane.

257
00:12:01,554 --> 00:12:03,374
Behind him, bags of mail.

258
00:12:03,624 --> 00:12:04,528
No radio.

259
00:12:04,669 --> 00:12:06,409
Can you imagine flying like that?

260
00:12:06,711 --> 00:12:10,113
He lands in Bombay on a mud flat at Juhu, by the sea.

261
00:12:10,427 --> 00:12:12,914
That flight is the start of an airline.

262
00:12:13,156 --> 00:12:14,141
Tata Airlines.

263
00:12:14,249 --> 00:12:15,605
Later, Air India.

264
00:12:15,913 --> 00:12:22,606
Six years after that flight, at thirty four, the unpaid apprentice is chairman of the whole Tata group.

265
00:12:22,877 --> 00:12:26,203
For twenty years he builds the airline, plane by plane.

266
00:12:26,442 --> 00:12:29,857
But in nineteen fifty three, the government takes over the airlines.

267
00:12:30,051 --> 00:12:32,532
J.R.D. thinks it's a mistake, and he says so.

268
00:12:32,837 --> 00:12:36,258
So what does he do when they ask him to stay on and run Air India?

269
00:12:36,556 --> 00:12:37,653
He says yes.

270
00:12:37,793 --> 00:12:41,487
And he does it without a salary, for twenty five years.

271
00:12:41,807 --> 00:12:47,765
Meanwhile, in nineteen sixty eight, almost quietly, Tata starts a small computer unit.

272
00:12:47,954 --> 00:12:51,081
Its first jobs are punched cards for its own companies.

273
00:12:51,346 --> 00:12:54,050
It's called Tata Consultancy Services.

274
00:12:54,334 --> 00:12:55,118
T.C.S.

275
00:12:55,325 --> 00:12:56,384
Remember that name.

276
00:12:56,561 --> 00:12:59,070
One day it will carry the whole empire.

277
00:12:59,603 --> 00:13:03,901
And in those years, J.R.D. gets a postcard he will never forget.

278
00:13:04,284 --> 00:13:06,035
April, nineteen seventy four.

279
00:13:06,306 --> 00:13:11,562
A young engineering student in Bangalore sees a job notice from a Tata company, Telco.

280
00:13:11,814 --> 00:13:13,532
At the bottom, one line.

281
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:15,889
Lady candidates need not apply.

282
00:13:16,270 --> 00:13:17,651
She could have kept quiet.

283
00:13:17,769 --> 00:13:18,794
Most people would.

284
00:13:19,065 --> 00:13:21,067
Instead, she writes a postcard.

285
00:13:21,237 --> 00:13:23,391
To the man at the top of the Tata group.

286
00:13:23,585 --> 00:13:27,648
How can a company like Telco treat people differently because of their gender?

287
00:13:27,922 --> 00:13:29,464
Less than ten days later, a telegram arrives.

288
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:30,698
Come for an interview.

289
00:13:30,858 --> 00:13:32,591
In Pune.

290
00:13:32,806 --> 00:13:33,895
She gets the job.

291
00:13:33,999 --> 00:13:37,178
She becomes the first woman to work on Telco's shop floor.

292
00:13:37,427 --> 00:13:38,466
You may know her name.

293
00:13:38,620 --> 00:13:39,522
Sudha Murty.

294
00:13:39,796 --> 00:13:42,293
But not every Tata story ends kindly.

295
00:13:42,581 --> 00:13:44,543
February, nineteen seventy eight.

296
00:13:44,744 --> 00:13:47,569
A new government removes J.R.D. from Air India.

297
00:13:47,821 --> 00:13:49,350
He is not even told directly.

298
00:13:49,524 --> 00:13:51,805
He hears it from the man who is replacing him.

299
00:13:52,059 --> 00:13:54,625
That night, someone asks how he feels.

300
00:13:54,832 --> 00:13:59,603
He says, I feel as you would feel if your favourite child was taken away.

301
00:14:00,972 --> 00:14:02,374
Nineteen ninety one.

302
00:14:02,618 --> 00:14:07,727
India comes close to running out of foreign money, and the country opens its doors to business.

303
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:12,067
That same year, J.R.D. hands over to a quiet, private man.

304
00:14:12,379 --> 00:14:13,256
Ratan Tata.

305
00:14:13,599 --> 00:14:15,524
Yes, he was born a Tata.

306
00:14:15,766 --> 00:14:17,217
But look at how he started.

307
00:14:17,446 --> 00:14:21,106
His parents separated when he was ten, and his grandmother raised him.

308
00:14:21,354 --> 00:14:23,249
He studied architecture in America.

309
00:14:23,543 --> 00:14:26,445
Then his grandmother fell ill, and he came home.

310
00:14:26,743 --> 00:14:28,106
His first years at Tata?

311
00:14:28,336 --> 00:14:29,318
On the shop floor.

312
00:14:29,575 --> 00:14:35,594
At the steel plant in Jamshedpur, he spends two years shovelling limestone and tending the blast furnace.

313
00:14:35,792 --> 00:14:40,794
The man who would one day run Tata started next to the furnace, with the workers.

314
00:14:40,954 --> 00:14:42,041
Can you picture that?

315
00:14:42,220 --> 00:14:44,542
Then he makes his first big bet.

316
00:14:44,745 --> 00:14:47,490
A car designed in India, by Indians.

317
00:14:47,642 --> 00:14:50,568
The Indica, unveiled in nineteen ninety eight.

318
00:14:50,722 --> 00:14:53,648
About one lakh fifteen thousand people book it.

319
00:14:53,894 --> 00:14:55,451
Then the complaints start.

320
00:14:55,702 --> 00:14:56,341
Quality.

321
00:14:56,544 --> 00:14:57,045
Power.

322
00:14:57,278 --> 00:15:02,793
So in nineteen ninety nine, Ratan Tata and his team fly to Detroit, to the Ford Motor Company.

323
00:15:02,921 --> 00:15:04,405
To sell the car business.

324
00:15:04,570 --> 00:15:07,125
Tata veteran Pravin Kadle was in that room.

325
00:15:07,294 --> 00:15:12,012
According to him, the Ford people asked why Tata had started a car business at all.

326
00:15:12,170 --> 00:15:15,335
And buying it, they said, would be doing Tata a favour.

327
00:15:15,509 --> 00:15:16,802
The deal is off.

328
00:15:17,079 --> 00:15:18,709
They fly back the same evening.

329
00:15:18,874 --> 00:15:23,116
Kadle says Ratan Tata hardly spoke, the whole ninety minute flight.

330
00:15:23,356 --> 00:15:24,568
You know that silence.

331
00:15:24,718 --> 00:15:27,190
The ride home after an interview goes wrong.

332
00:15:27,501 --> 00:15:28,865
Hold on to that silence.

333
00:15:29,393 --> 00:15:30,866
So what does Tata do?

334
00:15:31,221 --> 00:15:32,344
It does not sell.

335
00:15:32,586 --> 00:15:33,829
It fixes the car.

336
00:15:33,997 --> 00:15:35,753
And it starts buying.

337
00:15:36,079 --> 00:15:39,508
Then, in two thousand seven, the biggest bet of all.

338
00:15:39,767 --> 00:15:45,866
Tata Steel wins Corus, the company that included British Steel, for about twelve billion dollars.

339
00:15:46,096 --> 00:15:49,015
Remember Upcott, the man who would eat every rail?

340
00:15:49,223 --> 00:15:54,614
A hundred years later, an Indian steel company has bought what was once British Steel.

341
00:15:54,861 --> 00:15:58,085
But the next year tests Ratan Tata like no other.

342
00:15:58,312 --> 00:15:59,705
His proudest moment.

343
00:15:59,868 --> 00:16:01,056
His biggest deal.

344
00:16:01,297 --> 00:16:02,670
His hardest defeat.

345
00:16:02,926 --> 00:16:04,875
And his darkest night.

346
00:16:06,682 --> 00:16:07,421
January.

347
00:16:07,635 --> 00:16:08,254
Delhi.

348
00:16:08,496 --> 00:16:11,956
Ratan Tata drives a tiny car into the television lights.

349
00:16:12,187 --> 00:16:12,982
The Nano.

350
00:16:13,208 --> 00:16:15,166
A car for one lakh rupees.

351
00:16:15,309 --> 00:16:17,064
Costs have gone up, he says.

352
00:16:17,182 --> 00:16:19,259
But a promise is a promise.

353
00:16:19,422 --> 00:16:21,164
He said later what moved him.

354
00:16:21,347 --> 00:16:23,548
Indian families riding on scooters.

355
00:16:23,673 --> 00:16:25,772
Four or five people, on one scooter.

356
00:16:25,938 --> 00:16:27,073
You've seen that family.

357
00:16:27,265 --> 00:16:29,320
Maybe you were the child sitting in front.

358
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:31,852
He wanted to give that family a roof.

359
00:16:32,192 --> 00:16:32,857
March.

360
00:16:33,004 --> 00:16:35,573
Ford is selling two famous British brands.

361
00:16:35,705 --> 00:16:37,279
Jaguar and Land Rover.

362
00:16:37,462 --> 00:16:38,864
And who do you think buys them?

363
00:16:39,161 --> 00:16:39,683
Tata.

364
00:16:39,823 --> 00:16:42,132
About two point three billion dollars.

365
00:16:42,203 --> 00:16:47,780
And according to Pravin Kadle, Bill Ford told Ratan Tata, you are doing us a big favour.

366
00:16:47,996 --> 00:16:50,066
Ford has never confirmed either line.

367
00:16:50,204 --> 00:16:51,690
But the deal is real.

368
00:16:51,919 --> 00:16:52,602
October.

369
00:16:52,751 --> 00:16:59,045
The Nano factory at Singur, in West Bengal, has faced months of protests over the farmland taken to build it.

370
00:16:59,191 --> 00:17:02,572
Ratan Tata pulls out, and moves the factory to Gujarat.

371
00:17:02,771 --> 00:17:04,250
And then, November.

372
00:17:04,784 --> 00:17:07,731
The twenty sixth of November, two thousand eight.

373
00:17:08,028 --> 00:17:08,625
Night.

374
00:17:09,084 --> 00:17:10,935
Terrorists storm the Taj.

375
00:17:11,146 --> 00:17:12,427
Jamsetji's hotel.

376
00:17:12,725 --> 00:17:14,806
The one he opened in nineteen oh three.

377
00:17:15,167 --> 00:17:17,162
Inside, the staff could run.

378
00:17:17,377 --> 00:17:19,040
They know every back exit.

379
00:17:19,340 --> 00:17:21,976
But many of them stay, to get the guests out.

380
00:17:22,275 --> 00:17:23,754
These are ordinary people.

381
00:17:23,973 --> 00:17:25,351
Waiters.

382
00:17:25,349 --> 00:17:26,710
Cooks.

383
00:17:26,923 --> 00:17:27,914
Managers on a salary.

384
00:17:28,195 --> 00:17:29,412
People like us.

385
00:17:29,406 --> 00:17:32,860
A banquet manager, just twenty four, locks the doors and turns off the lights.

386
00:17:33,054 --> 00:17:36,953
She keeps more than sixty guests hidden and silent in the dark.

387
00:17:37,196 --> 00:17:40,633
In the kitchens, young chefs lead guests out through the back.

388
00:17:40,841 --> 00:17:42,827
A number of them are killed doing it.

389
00:17:43,108 --> 00:17:48,552
The general manager, Karambir Singh Kang, has his wife and two young sons inside the hotel.

390
00:17:48,852 --> 00:17:50,024
They die in the fire.

391
00:17:50,194 --> 00:17:54,781
And when Ratan Tata asks him to go and rest, he begs to stay and help.

392
00:17:54,956 --> 00:17:57,424
At least thirty one people die at the Taj.

393
00:17:57,630 --> 00:17:59,660
The fighting lasts three days.

394
00:17:59,867 --> 00:18:03,381
Twenty five days later, the Taj opens its doors again.

395
00:18:03,624 --> 00:18:06,946
The old palace wing takes almost two years to rebuild.

396
00:18:08,298 --> 00:18:10,591
Big bets come with big bills.

397
00:18:10,912 --> 00:18:14,473
A year after the Corus deal, the world economy crashes.

398
00:18:14,743 --> 00:18:16,852
Years of losses follow in Britain.

399
00:18:17,123 --> 00:18:22,891
And in twenty twenty four, the last blast furnaces at Port Talbot, in Wales, are shut down.

400
00:18:23,317 --> 00:18:24,649
Was Corus worth it?

401
00:18:24,994 --> 00:18:26,697
People still argue about that.

402
00:18:27,054 --> 00:18:27,813
And the Nano?

403
00:18:28,183 --> 00:18:31,555
People did not want to be seen in the cheapest car in the world.

404
00:18:31,785 --> 00:18:35,213
Tata itself admitted that calling it cheap was a mistake.

405
00:18:35,498 --> 00:18:37,435
Production ended in twenty eighteen.

406
00:18:37,789 --> 00:18:40,268
So does Tata always get it right?

407
00:18:40,625 --> 00:18:41,141
No.

408
00:18:41,414 --> 00:18:43,876
More than once, it gets it badly wrong.

409
00:18:44,190 --> 00:18:50,946
In twenty sixteen, the man who took over from Ratan Tata, Cyrus Mistry, is removed in a bitter boardroom fight.

410
00:18:51,142 --> 00:18:55,089
It goes all the way to the Supreme Court, which sides with Tata Sons.

411
00:18:55,318 --> 00:18:57,597
The new chairman is N. Chandrasekaran.

412
00:18:57,816 --> 00:19:00,001
He joined T.C.S. as a young engineer.

413
00:19:00,276 --> 00:19:01,253
And T.C.S.?

414
00:19:01,309 --> 00:19:03,101
That little punched card unit?

415
00:19:03,318 --> 00:19:04,995
It's now the engine.

416
00:19:05,256 --> 00:19:13,071
Last year, of all the profit Tata's companies sent up to Tata Sons, about eighty seven percent came from T.C.S. alone.

417
00:19:13,366 --> 00:19:17,222
Then, in twenty twenty two, the favourite child comes home.

418
00:19:17,527 --> 00:19:22,312
Air India returns to Tata, sixty nine years after it was taken away.

419
00:19:22,633 --> 00:19:24,716
J.R.D. did not live to see it.

420
00:19:25,044 --> 00:19:27,637
But there's one more moment we have to stop for.

421
00:19:28,005 --> 00:19:29,638
June, twenty twenty five.

422
00:19:30,044 --> 00:19:35,659
Air India flight one seven one, bound for London, crashes just after take off from Ahmedabad.

423
00:19:35,921 --> 00:19:38,278
Two hundred and sixty people are killed.

424
00:19:38,533 --> 00:19:42,701
The final report is not out yet, and the cause has not been established.

425
00:19:43,007 --> 00:19:45,647
We remember every life that was lost.

426
00:19:46,847 --> 00:19:47,442
So.

427
00:19:47,700 --> 00:19:51,708
Back to the bank in nineteen twenty four, and the question we started with.

428
00:19:51,952 --> 00:19:54,653
Why is no Tata on top of a rich list?

429
00:19:54,872 --> 00:19:57,800
Because the Tata family doesn't own Tata.

430
00:19:58,135 --> 00:20:00,526
Tata Sons sits at the top of the group.

431
00:20:00,719 --> 00:20:04,069
And about two thirds of it belongs to charitable trusts.

432
00:20:04,181 --> 00:20:07,669
And the biggest of them is the Sir Dorabji Tata Trust.

433
00:20:07,899 --> 00:20:10,024
The trust of the man with the diamond.

434
00:20:10,257 --> 00:20:13,140
It holds about twenty eight percent of Tata Sons.

435
00:20:13,376 --> 00:20:14,682
And who owns the rest?

436
00:20:14,836 --> 00:20:21,473
Most of it belongs to the Shapoorji Pallonji group, owned by the Mistry family, and to Tata's own companies.

437
00:20:21,667 --> 00:20:27,053
So when you buy that salt, or that tea, a share of the profit flows up to those trusts.

438
00:20:27,134 --> 00:20:30,762
And out, into hospitals, research and scholarships.

439
00:20:30,930 --> 00:20:34,966
For almost a hundred years, that design held the empire together.

440
00:20:35,229 --> 00:20:39,327
Then, in October twenty twenty four, Ratan Tata died.

441
00:20:39,477 --> 00:20:44,695
His body lay at a hall in South Mumbai, and thousands came.

442
00:20:44,938 --> 00:20:45,835
Students.

443
00:20:45,882 --> 00:20:46,951
Ordinary people.

444
00:20:47,089 --> 00:20:48,903
Strangers who had never met him.

445
00:20:49,163 --> 00:20:51,046
He was given a state funeral.

446
00:20:51,213 --> 00:20:54,526
His half brother, Noel Tata, now leads the trusts.

447
00:20:54,648 --> 00:20:58,182
And in twenty twenty six, the cracks are out in the open.

448
00:20:58,372 --> 00:20:59,939
So what is the fight about?

449
00:21:00,149 --> 00:21:02,854
Who runs Tata, and who decides that.

450
00:21:03,013 --> 00:21:07,511
This September, the Tata Sons board voted to give Chandrasekaran another term.

451
00:21:07,712 --> 00:21:09,507
Noel Tata voted no.

452
00:21:09,711 --> 00:21:12,328
The trusts say that decision doesn't count.

453
00:21:12,568 --> 00:21:14,522
Tata Sons says it does.

454
00:21:15,046 --> 00:21:16,390
And here's the catch.

455
00:21:16,613 --> 00:21:20,436
To the R.B.I., Tata Sons looks like a big finance company.

456
00:21:20,739 --> 00:21:24,032
And big finance companies must sell shares to the public.

457
00:21:24,320 --> 00:21:26,181
Tata Sons asked to be let off.

458
00:21:26,420 --> 00:21:27,988
The R.B.I. said no.

459
00:21:28,303 --> 00:21:31,158
As this film goes out, that fight is not over.

460
00:21:31,455 --> 00:21:32,172
Who wins?

461
00:21:32,312 --> 00:21:33,529
I honestly don't know.

462
00:21:33,762 --> 00:21:34,825
Nobody does yet.

463
00:21:35,155 --> 00:21:38,447
But step back for a moment, and look at who built this.

464
00:21:38,676 --> 00:21:42,223
A priest's son whose family nearly lost everything.

465
00:21:42,480 --> 00:21:44,697
Eight thousand ordinary savers.

466
00:21:44,857 --> 00:21:47,384
A husband and wife who pledged their diamond.

467
00:21:47,590 --> 00:21:49,864
An apprentice who worked without pay.

468
00:21:50,062 --> 00:21:52,026
A young engineer with a postcard.

469
00:21:52,188 --> 00:21:55,019
A man shovelling limestone next to the furnace.

470
00:21:55,324 --> 00:21:56,510
What do they have in common?

471
00:21:56,754 --> 00:22:00,596
Every one of them, at some point, put something on the line.

472
00:22:00,895 --> 00:22:01,762
Savings.

473
00:22:01,992 --> 00:22:02,607
Pride.

474
00:22:02,827 --> 00:22:03,642
A diamond.

475
00:22:03,834 --> 00:22:05,697
That part needs no fortune.

476
00:22:05,971 --> 00:22:07,567
That part is yours.

477
00:22:07,815 --> 00:22:11,338
And a hundred years later, the question is still the same one.

478
00:22:11,565 --> 00:22:14,037
Who should a company like this really belong to?

479
00:22:14,386 --> 00:22:15,234
What do you think?

480
00:22:15,493 --> 00:22:16,584
Tell me in the comments.

481
00:22:17,951 --> 00:22:19,851
This is Built on Bharat.

482
00:22:19,998 --> 00:22:23,150
The empires, and the habits, that built a nation.

483
00:22:23,377 --> 00:22:25,292
Next in Empires of India.

484
00:22:25,515 --> 00:22:29,586
How did a group of farmers in a small Gujarat town build Amul?
